<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Ganja Khanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1747 CE – 1804 CE · capital Ganja</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A khanate centred on the city of Ganja whose last khan, Javad Khan, died defending it against the Russian storm of 1804.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah's</a> death in 1747, Shahverdi Khan of the Ziyadoghlu branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> tribe, whose family had long governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganja-azerbaijan">Ganja</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a>, made Ganja an independent khanate. It covered the fertile plain between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kura-river">Kura</a> and the Lesser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The khans ruled a town of craftsmen and merchants and surrounding villages producing grain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and fruit. Pressed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/karabakh-khanate">Karabakh Khanate</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/georgia">Georgia</a>, the khanate paid tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erekle-ii">Erekle II</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kartli-kakheti">Kingdom of Kartli-Kakheti</a> for decades. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/javad-khan">Javad Khan</a>, who ruled from 1786, rebuilt its strength and sided with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In January 1804 Russian troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pavel-tsitsianov">Pavel Tsitsianov</a> stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganja-azerbaijan">Ganja</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/javad-khan">Javad Khan</a> was killed with his son. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> annexed the khanate and renamed the city Elizavetpol; it regained the name Ganja in the 20th century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ganja-khanate.jpg" alt="Ganja Khanate" width="320" height="231"><figcaption>Image: Seymur06 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Ganja_Khanate.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1747 CE – 1804 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ganja</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Shahverdi Khan, Javad Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Azerbaijani; Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Khanate of Ganja, Ganja</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1776&amp;focus=ganja-khanate" aria-label="Ganja Khanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5816 725 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ganja Khanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6136 981L6298 1144M6136 981L6091 947M6136 981L6487 457"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6298 1144h0M6091 947h0M6487 457h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6136 981h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6136 981h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ganja. <a href="/atlas?year=1776&amp;focus=ganja-khanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
